1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome to the Seal Hall, also known as the Seal Beach, or the Hall of Two Beaches.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:11,000
This is the final section of the tourist route through the Grata del Boe Marino.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
The name comes from the presence of sand.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
As you can see, we are no longer walking on an artificial walkway, but on the cave's actual floor.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,000
The hall is dedicated to Monaco's Monaco's, the Mediterranean monk Seal.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:36,000
It was right here in the 1970s that one of the last specimens still present in the Gulf of Orasay was observed, a mother with her pup.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:48,000
These small internal beaches offered an ideal refuge, a sheltered silent place, safe from storm swells, and informer times from human presence as well.

8
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
They were perfect spaces for resting and nursing the young.

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:01,000
This is not simply a hall of the cave. It is a place that preserves one of the last testimonies of the monk Seal's presence in these waters.

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Among all the halls on the tourist route of the Grata del Boe Marino, this is the one that changes most from year to year, precisely because of the active presence of the river.

11
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
The sand beneath our feet does not come from the sea, but from the underground river.

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
During winter, depending on the amount of rainfall, the river can increase its flow considerably.

13
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:35,000
At certain times, it even covers much of the floor, bringing in new sand or carrying some away, and slightly altering the morphology of the space.

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:43,000
What for us marks the end of the tourist route is, in reality, also the beginning of the spiliological section of the system.

15
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
From this point onwards, the cave continues and branches out for kilometers.

16
00:01:49,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Connecting to the Grata de Supalu, the Grata de Sumalente, and the Grata de Montelongos, forming the approximately 75 kilometers of explored passages known today.

17
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The spiliological section consists of large halls, rich in fresh water, wide internal beaches, imposing formations, and numerous sums.

18
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Environment's completely filled with water that can only be navigated by experienced cavers using scuba equipment and tanks.

19
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
The visit ends here.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Before beginning the return journey, you will be directed according to your next destination.

21
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Calaluna, the mini cruise, or the port of Calagononi.

22
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:44,000
Each group will have a specific exit order, so it is essential that you listen carefully to the guide's instructions, so as not to risk missing your boat.

23
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Thank you for choosing to visit the Grata de Albuay Marino.

24
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:57,000
We hope this journey has moved you and allowed you to discover something more about this extraordinary territory and the underground world it conceals.

